let __v_25 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"elephant\",\"Grass, leaves, bark and fruit: a big bull eats up to 150 kg of plants a day and drinks around 100 litres of water.\"],[\"lion\",\"Meat, and only meat - zebra, wildebeest, antelope and buffalo, taken by the females in a group hunt. A lion eats around 5 to 7 kg a day and can go a week without.\"],[\"tiger\",\"Meat: deer, wild pig and wild cattle, hunted alone and mostly at night. A tiger can eat up to 40 kg in one sitting and then go several days without.\"],[\"cheetah\",\"Meat, and it has to be fresh: hares, young antelope and birds, caught in a sprint and eaten quickly before a lion or a hyena takes it. A cheetah eats around 3 kg a day.\"],[\"giraffe\",\"Leaves, mostly acacia: the tongue and lips are tough enough to strip thorny twigs without harm. A giraffe eats around 34 kg of leaves a day and drinks only every few days.\"],[\"zebra\",\"Grass, almost all of it: a zebra grazes up to 16 hours a day and needs water every day or two.\"],[\"rhino\",\"Grass for the white rhino, which grazes with its head down; the black rhino browses bushes and leaves with its lip hooked round a twig. Both eat around 50 kg a day.\"],[\"hippo\",\"Grass, and almost all of it at night on land: a hippo can eat 40 kg in a night and spends the day in the water.\"],[\"gorilla\",\"Plants - leaves, shoots, stems and fruit - around 20 kg a day for a big male, with the odd insect. A mountain gorilla eats very little meat.\"],[\"chimpanzee\",\"Mostly fruit, plus leaves, seeds and insects, and now and then meat from a monkey it has hunted, which is a group affair with a lot of noise.\"],[\"panda\",\"Bamboo, almost nothing else: 12 to 38 kg a day, because bamboo gives so little energy that a panda eats for up to 14 hours of it.\"],[\"koala\",\"Eucalyptus leaves only, and only a few species of them: a koala eats about half a kilo a day and sleeps 18 to 20 hours.\"],[\"kangaroo\",\"Grass and leaves, grazed at dawn and dusk; a kangaroo needs little water and gets most of it from the plants.\"],[\"whale\",\"Krill and small fish for a baleen whale, strained through the plates in its mouth; a toothed whale takes fish and squid.\"],[\"blue whale\",\"Krill, and up to 4 tonnes of it a day in the feeding season, strained through the baleen plates; a blue whale eats little or nothing while it migrates.\"],[\"dolphin\",\"Fish and squid, swallowed whole or shaken apart first: a dolphin eats around 10 to 20 kg of fish a day.\"],[\"shark\",\"Fish, squid, seals and carrion, by species: a great white hunts seals, a basking shark strains plankton through its gills, and a nurse shark sucks shellfish off the seabed.\"],[\"octopus\",\"Crabs, shellfish and small fish, cracked open with its beak and pulled apart; an octopus hunts at night and can taste with its arms.\"],[\"penguin\",\"Fish, squid and krill, caught by diving: an emperor penguin can dive to 500 metres and stay under for 20 minutes.\"],[\"tortoise\",\"Leaves, weeds and flowers - dandelion, plantain, clover, bindweed - for a Mediterranean tortoise, with no fruit and no dog food. A pet tortoise gets a pile about the size of its shell, once a day.\"],[\"crocodile\",\"Meat: fish, birds, turtles and anything that comes to drink, taken in one lunge and swallowed in pieces. A crocodile can go months between meals.\"],[\"eagle\",\"Meat: rabbits, fish, birds or carrion, by species, taken with the talons and torn up at a perch.\"],[\"ostrich\",\"Mostly plants - grass, seeds, leaves and flowers - with the odd insect or lizard; an ostrich has no teeth and swallows small stones to grind its food.\"],[\"horse\",\"Grass or hay, most of the day: a horse eats about 2% of its body weight a day, so 10 kg of hay for a 500 kg horse, in small amounts through the day, with water always available.\"],[\"greyhound\",\"A complete dog food, and less than its size suggests: a retired racer of about 30 kg eats 400 to 500 g a day, split into two meals.\"],[\"bee\",\"Nectar and pollen: the adults live on nectar, and the larvae on pollen and honey. A colony can bring in 20 to 30 kg of pollen in a year.\"],[\"ant\",\"Almost anything - other insects, seeds, nectar and the honeydew off aphids. Some ants farm aphids for it, and some grow fungus on leaves instead.\"],[\"cow\",\"Grass and hay, and a lot of it: a dairy cow eats around 20 kg of dry matter a day, which is most of a bale of hay, and drinks 60 to 100 litres of water.\"],[\"sheep\",\"Grass, mostly, plus the clover and weeds in it: a sheep eats around 2 kg of dry matter a day and spends 8 to 10 hours of it grazing.\"],[\"pig\",\"Almost anything, which is what an omnivore means: a pig eats 2 to 3 kg of feed a day and roots for grubs, roots and fallen fruit with a nose built to dig. It cannot sweat, so it wallows to cool down. Kitchen scraps and any meat are illegal to feed a pig in the UK, and that rule is what keeps swine fever out. A wild boar, the same animal, lives on roots, acorns, grubs and carrion over a much bigger range.\"],[\"chicken\",\"Layers pellets or mash as the base, about 120 g a day per hen, plus grass, weeds, insects and vegetable scraps; grit and crushed oyster shell keep the eggshells hard. Bread and corn are treats rather than feed, and avocado, citrus peel, salt and anything mouldy are not for them at all.\"],[\"goat\",\"Browse first, not grass: brambles, hedge, leaves, bark, weeds and thistles, then hay - about 2 kg of hay a day plus a mineral lick and water. A goat has a stomach for roughage, so rich grass or a lot of grain makes it ill, and it will strip a garden to the fence line given the chance.\"],[\"pony\",\"Grass and hay, and less of it than people think: a 300 kg pony needs about 6 kg of hay a day in dry matter, plus water and a salt lick, and a native pony on good grass needs nothing else - which is why a fat pony is the one that gets laminitis in spring. Hard feed is for a pony in work, not for one standing in a field.\"],[\"donkey\",\"Straw and hay, and less than a pony of the same size: a donkey has a slower digestive system and gets fat on good grass, so the work is keeping it thin with coarse hay and a bare paddock rather than feeding it up.\"],[\"lizard\",\"Insects for nearly all of them - crickets, locusts and mealworms, dusted with a calcium powder - and the green ones take leaves and veg instead. It needs the right heat to digest at all, so the lamp matters as much as the food.\"],[\"polar bear\",\"Seals, and mostly the ringed seal: a polar bear waits at a breathing hole and takes the seal as it surfaces, then eats the blubber first. It can go days between meals and swims long distances between patches of ice.\"]]}")?;
frame.store("EATS", __v_25.clone())?;
