let __v_499 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bat\",\"bats\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"blind\",\"blindness\",\"see\",\"seeing\",\"sight\"]},\"Bats are not blind: they see about as well as a cat in dim light. What makes them special is echolocation, squeaks too high to hear that bounce back and tell them where things are in the dark.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"zebra\",\"zebras\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"stripe\",\"stripes\",\"striped\"]},\"Nobody knows for certain, and the best evidence points at flies and heat rather than camouflage: the stripes seem to confuse biting flies and help the animal lose heat, and the pattern on each zebra is as individual as a fingerprint.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"penguin\",\"penguins\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"north pole\",\"arctic\",\"south pole\",\"antarctic\",\"where\",\"where do penguins live\",\"which pole\"]},\"Penguins live in the southern half of the world: Antarctica, and the coasts of South America, Africa, Australia and New Zealand. There are none at the North Pole; the birds there are puffins, which are not penguins.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"penguin\",\"penguins\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"hold their breath\",\"hold its breath\",\"breath\",\"dive\",\"diving\",\"deep\",\"swim\",\"swimming\"]},\"An emperor penguin can hold its breath for around 20 minutes and dive past 500 metres; most dives are one to five minutes and less than 100 metres.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"penguin\",\"penguins\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"warm\",\"cold\",\"freeze\",\"survive\",\"huddle\",\"huddles\"]},\"Four things: a layer of blubber, feathers packed so tightly that water never reaches the skin, feet and flippers with a heat-exchange that keeps the blood warm, and huddling. In a huddle each bird takes a turn on the freezing outside.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ant\",\"ants\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"winter\",\"cold\",\"survive\",\"survives\",\"hibernate\",\"hibernation\"]},\"They slow down and go deep: a colony moves to the lowest, frost-free part of the nest, clusters together, and eats stored food, with the queen in the middle. They do not truly hibernate, they just keep still until it warms up.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ant\",\"ants\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"strong\",\"stronger\",\"strength\",\"carry\",\"lift\",\"how much can\"]},\"An ant carries 10 to 50 times its own body weight, and holds on with feet that grip smooth surfaces, which is why one can walk off with a whole crisp.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"octopus\",\"octopuses\",\"octopi\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"heart\",\"hearts\",\"how many\"]},\"Three: two pump blood through the gills and one pumps it round the rest of the body. Their blood is blue, because it uses copper rather than iron to carry oxygen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"octopus\",\"octopuses\",\"octopi\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"arm\",\"arms\",\"leg\",\"legs\",\"how many\"]},\"Eight arms, and two rows of suckers on each, about 2,000 suckers in all. Each one can taste what it touches.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"fish\",\"fishes\",\"goldfish\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"breathe\",\"breathing\",\"gills\",\"underwater\",\"under water\",\"oxygen\",\"need oxygen\",\"oxygen in the water\",\"air\"]},\"They take oxygen out of the water instead of the air: water goes in at the mouth, passes over the gills, and the gills pull the oxygen out and pass the carbon dioxide back. A few fish, like bettas and gouramis, can also gulp air from the surface.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"fish\",\"fishes\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"feel pain\",\"pain\",\"suffer\",\"suffering\"]},\"The evidence says probably yes: fish have nerves and opioid receptors, and they learn to avoid a place where they were hurt. Scientists argued about it for years, and it is why handling rules for angling have tightened.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"dog\",\"dogs\",\"puppy\",\"puppies\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"wag\",\"wagging\",\"wags\",\"tail\",\"tails\"]},\"It is a signal, and the tail is not always saying the same thing: a loose, wide wag with a loose body means happy, a fast low wag can mean nerves, and a stiff high wag is usually a warning. Studies find a wag that swings more to the dog's right goes with a friendly meeting.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"spider\",\"spiders\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"how many legs\",\"legs\",\"eyes\",\"how many eyes\"]},\"Spiders have eight legs and usually eight eyes, in rows across the front of the head. They have silk glands in the abdomen and spin the web out with their back legs.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bat\",\"bats\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"sleep\",\"sleeps\",\"hibernate\",\"hibernation\",\"upside down\"]},\"Bats sleep upside down, hanging by their feet, and many hibernate through the winter that way. Hanging upside down means a fall is a drop of a few centimetres, and taking off is just letting go.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"beaver\",\"beavers\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"dam\",\"dams\",\"why\",\"build\"]},\"A beaver builds a dam to hold the water deep enough that the entrance to its lodge is under the surface, which keeps out foxes, wolves and bears. The pond also means it can swim to food in winter instead of walking over land.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"swallow\",\"swallows\",\"swift\",\"swifts\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"sleep\",\"sleeps\",\"fly\",\"flying\",\"land\"]},\"Swifts sleep on the wing, climbing to where they can glide slowly for hours at a time. Some stay airborne for months, and they only land to nest.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chameleon\",\"chameleons\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"change colour\",\"colour\",\"color\",\"why\"]},\"They stretch or squeeze layers of pigment cells in the skin. It is mostly mood and temperature rather than camouflage: a calm chameleon is often green, and a cross one goes bright or dark.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"elephant\",\"elephants\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"trunk\",\"why\",\"how many bones\"]},\"An elephant's trunk has no bones at all and around 40,000 muscles, which is why it can pick up a single peanut or lift a tree. Elephants greet by touching trunks.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"hibernate\",\"hibernates\",\"hibernating\",\"hibernation\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"why\",\"how\",\"animals\",\"bear\",\"bears\",\"hedgehog\",\"survive\",\"winter\"]},\"Hibernation is a way of getting through a season with no food: the animal's temperature, heart rate and breathing drop, and it lives off the fat it laid down in autumn. A bear's heart can slow from 40 beats a minute to 8, and a hedgehog turns almost cold to the touch. A bat or a hedgehog woken in winter has spent a lot of energy for nothing, so leave one be.\"]}]}")?;
